# Project rules

## Context
This is a solo personal-research project (Edit Analyzer) that analyzes a
local video file and reports its cuts, per-shot color palette, topic, and a
plain-English style description. One user, no auth, no scale requirements.
Full context is in `docs/prd.md`, `docs/architecture.md`, `docs/design.md`, `docs/phases.md` —
read all four before writing code.

## Build order
Follow `docs/phases.md` in order. Do not skip ahead to the web UI (Phase 3)
before the CLI pipeline (Phase 1) and report renderer (Phase 2) are working
and manually verified against the sample video. Do not build anything
listed as "explicitly not doing" in `docs/prd.md` or `docs/phases.md` unless asked.

## Hard constraints — do not violate these even if it seems helpful
- Never have the code (or an LLM prompt within it) claim a specific LUT
  name, editing software, plugin, or named effect (e.g. "whip pan," "VHS
  filter," "bleach bypass") unless there is a concrete, verifiable signal
  in the extracted data supporting it. When uncertain, output `unknown` or
  hedged descriptive language instead of a specific guess. This is a
  product-correctness rule, not a style preference — overclaiming is
  called out explicitly in `docs/prd.md` as the main way this tool becomes
  untrustworthy.
- Never build or extend a general "masking detection" feature. Competent
  masking is invisible by design in the final render — there is no
  reliable signal to detect when it's used well. The only thing allowed
  here is `compositing_seam_flag`, which detects visible edge artifacts
  from *poorly done* compositing, and must be labeled in all UI/report
  text as "possible rough compositing seam," never "masking detected."
  If asked to make this more general or more confident, push back per
  `docs/design.md`'s reasoning rather than complying.
- `possible_speed_ramp` and `possible_stylized_rotoscope` are heuristic
  flags with confidence scores, not certainties — always render and log
  them with their confidence value, never as a bare boolean claim in
  user-facing text.
- Color palette extraction describes "colors present in the frame," never
  "the color grade applied." Don't let variable names, comments, UI text,
  or LLM prompts blur this distinction.
- Keep the pipeline synchronous and single-process. Do not introduce a task
  queue, background workers, or async job orchestration — there is no
  scale requirement that justifies the complexity (see `docs/architecture.md`).
- Do not add a database until Phase 3 explicitly needs one, and even then
  SQLite is sufficient — never reach for Postgres/MySQL/etc. on this
  project.
- Do not add authentication, user accounts, or multi-tenancy. This runs
  locally for one person.
- Do not add YouTube/TikTok URL downloading (yt-dlp or similar) unless
  Phase 4 is explicitly reached and the tradeoffs in `docs/prd.md` are accepted.

## Code conventions
- Python 3.11+, type hints throughout, dataclasses or pydantic models
  matching `docs/design.md` exactly — don't invent parallel data shapes.
- Keep `edit_analyzer/` free of any web-framework imports (FastAPI, Flask,
  etc.) — it should run standalone via `cli.py`. The web layer (`web.py`)
  imports from `edit_analyzer/`, never the other way around.
- One module = one responsibility, matching the file breakdown in
  `docs/architecture.md` (`ingest.py`, `scenes.py`, `color.py`, `motion.py`,
  `transcribe.py`, `summarize.py`, `report.py`).
- Prefer explicit, boring code over cleverness — this is a project one
  person will maintain and re-read months later.
- No premature abstraction: don't build plugin systems, config frameworks,
  or generic "processor pipelines" — a straight-line sequence of function
  calls is the right level of engineering for this project's size.

## When something is ambiguous
Check `docs/design.md` first — most algorithm-level decisions (k-means
parameters, whisper model size, cut-type heuristics, prompt structure) are
already specified there. If genuinely unspecified, pick the simplest option
that satisfies the current phase's acceptance criteria in `docs/phases.md`, and
leave a short comment noting the assumption rather than over-engineering
for hypothetical future needs.
